"""
What an agent may reach once its configuration and token are read
, as a policy of Standard.Sandbox: pledge and unveil on OpenBSD,
Landlock and seccomp on Linux.

- The promises: stdio rpath wpath cpath flock inet dns.
- Read: the CA file, the certificate, the key, /etc/resolv.conf,
  /etc/hosts, and the directory of each file of the configuration, so
  that a log rotated to a new file of the same name stays in reach
  (Landlock holds a file by its inode).
- Read, write and create: the directory of the state, where SQLite keeps
  its journal beside the database.
- Nothing else is granted; whatever lies in a granted directory is in
  reach, so the configuration and the token belong elsewhere.

    let policy: Policy := agentPolicy(&config);
    let report: Report := applyPolicy(policy);
"""
import Standard.Sandbox (
    Policy
);
import Roxie.Config (
    AgentConfig
);

module Roxie.Confine is
    """
    The promises of an agent, in the words of pledge.
    """
    function agentPromises(): Span[Nat8, Static]
        ensures spanLength(result) > 0;

    """
    The policy of the agent of config.  The directory of the state is
    the part of its path before the last /, and so is the directory of
    a file.  The root is never granted: the agent of a state or a file
    directly under / cannot reach it.
    The tests compare describePaths of it.

    No contract: a Policy is opaque and its count is read through a
    borrow, which a condition on the result cannot take.
    """
    generic [C: Region]
    function agentPolicy(config: &[AgentConfig, C]): Policy;
end module.
